# rope_float16b.cbtable
#   The CB contract for the resident ROPE triad (resident_rope_compute.c).
#
#     cb  0  Float16_b  2048 B   the activation, tile column c of a head
#     cb  1  Float16_b  2048 B   the PARTNER tile, ftr_partner_tile(c) of the same head
#     cb  2  Float16_b  2048 B   cos(angle) for column c, from the table
#     cb  3  Float16_b  2048 B   SIGNED sin(angle) for column c: -sin low half, +sin high
#     cb  4  Float16_b  2048 B   staging: in * cos
#     cb  5  Float16_b  2048 B   staging: partner * sin
#     cb 16  Float16_b  2048 B   output
#
#   The reader and writer must be built with tile_bytes=2048 to match. There is no
#   descriptor header on a data-movement core, so nothing checks that for you.
#
#   EVERY CB IS THE SAME FORMAT, and here that is a real decision rather than an accident:
#   the TABLE is a bf16 operand beside bf16 activations, so no reconfig_data_format() is
#   needed anywhere and the two staging buffers can be added without one either. A future
#   fp32 table (see ft_tt_op_rope.h on what bf16 costs the angle) would make cb 2 and cb 3 a
#   different format, and that is a mixed-format contract exactly like matmul_bfp8_b's —
#   normal, not exotic — but it would need the reconfig calls this kernel omits.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# dst_accum_mode 1 -- fp32 destination accumulation, ON
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# docs/tt_bringup_log.md §19: 13-35x accuracy for ZERO wall clock on the matmul, because
# these ops are data-movement bound. Every op written from here on turns it on unless it has
# a measured reason not to, and this one does not.
#
# What it buys HERE specifically: the two products are computed in fp32 in DST and then
# packed to bf16 before the add, so each rotated element carries exactly two bf16 roundings
# (one per product) plus one for the sum. With a 16-bit DST the products would also be
# rounded on the way INTO the accumulator, i.e. an extra rounding of a value that is about to
# be rounded again. This is not the matmul's running-sum failure mode — there is no K loop —
# so the effect is small and NOT measured here; the setting is free either way.
#
# DST capacity: one tile at a time, dst_register always 0, so the halved Float32 capacity
# cannot bind.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# math_fidelity 4 -- CHOSEN, same reason as everywhere else here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only level with an a-priori accuracy guarantee (more mantissa bits per product), and free
# on a data-movement-bound op (docs/tt_bringup_log.md §16.3, MEASURED on the matmul;
# INFERRED for this op). RoPE does even less math per byte than the matmul — three FPU
# passes over four tile streams — so if fidelity is free there it is free here.
#
#   Format: tools/emit_tensix_cb_formats.py
#   cb <index> <format> <tile_r> <tile_c> <face_r> <num_faces>

math_fidelity  4
approx         0
dst_accum_mode 1

cb  0  Float16_b 32 32 16 4
cb  1  Float16_b 32 32 16 4
cb  2  Float16_b 32 32 16 4
cb  3  Float16_b 32 32 16 4
cb  4  Float16_b 32 32 16 4
cb  5  Float16_b 32 32 16 4
cb 16  Float16_b 32 32 16 4
